A virtual environment creates an isolated Python environment for a project.

Why do we need venv?

Imagine you have two projects.

Project A — AI Chatbot
numpy 2.x
requests 2.x
fastapi

Project B — Old ML Project
numpy 1.x
requests 1.x

If you install everything globally:
Global Python
    ↓
numpy 1.x
numpy 2.x
???
You can get dependency conflicts.


With virtual environments:
Project A
└── venv
    └── numpy 2.x


Project B
└── venv
    └── numpy 1.x

No conflict.


Create a virtual environment

Create a project:

mkdir ai_project
cd ai_project

Now:

python -m venv .venv

This creates:

ai_project/
│
└── .venv/
Why .venv?

It's simply a common convention.

You could call it:

venv
environment
myenv

But .venv is very common in modern Python projects.

What does .venv contain?

You don't need to manually modify it.

Conceptually:

.venv/
│
├── Python environment
├── pip
├── installed packages
└── environment configuration


1.  Activate the environment — Windows

Since you're using Windows/PowerShell, you'll commonly use:

.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

After activation, your terminal should show something like:

(.venv) PS C:\...\ai_project>

The important part is:

(.venv)

That tells you the virtual environment is active.

2. Activate — Command Prompt

If you're using CMD instead of PowerShell:

.venv\Scripts\activate